  x53*uW1,             x58*uW2,             x70, x72.
  ```

  Notably, `x53,x58,x70,x72` all occur in the banked 13-variable free set.
  This is not a proof of properness, but it strengthens the NONEMPTY prior.

Therefore, on this prime and radical fiber,

```text
V(row22red) is empty
  iff V(I21 + <R2,R3,R4>) is empty.
```

The five auxiliary directions invisible to the NFs are free; `d1,d2` are
then recovered uniquely from the first two NFs.  Pivots and the four solved
deep directions are recovered afterward, with the six deep-kernel directions
remaining free as prescribed by the rank-four Schur step.

### 3.4 Acceleration ratings

| emission/workflow | rating | reason |
|---|---:|---|
| Ordinary solver input: 397 GB polynomials + six unreduced rows | **3/10** | About 290k input terms; an ordinary ideal solve may recompute the GB and all critical-pair structure.  Merely listing a GB is not a warm start. |
| Known-basis normal forms (`-n 6 -i`, after a toy/version gate) | **10/10** | This is the correct quick shot and completed locally in 0.76 s; Singular independently agrees. |
| True incremental solve from `G` plus three 10-term rows | **8/10** | Algebraically right, but engine support matters; a classical Singular first increment did not finish inside a three-minute pilot. |
| **Original 22v D21 fiber core + three 10-term rows** | **10/10, preferred** | Only 29 equations and **5,378 total terms**.  It lets the engine recover the known 397-element D21 basis from the 5,348-term core, previously an 11 s task, without ingesting the 10 MB basis. |

The headline “GB warm start” should therefore be refined:

> Use the banked GB to discover and certify the quotient rows; solve the
> sparse original D21 generators plus the three quotient obstructions.
